
---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Container Assignments"
  description="Learn how loot tables are assigned to containers and how container rewards are handled."
>
  <div class="doc-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">CONFIGURATION</span>
    <h1>Container Assignments</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Container assignments determine which loot table is used when a player
      opens a container. They connect your loot tables to the containers
      players discover throughout your world.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Understanding assignments</h2>
    <p>
      An assignment connects a container to a specific loot table. This lets
      you control which rewards are available from different containers
      without needing to create a separate loot system for every container.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
      <h3>Why use assignments?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Give different containers access to different loot tables.</li>
        <li>Organize rewards around different areas or dimensions.</li>
        <li>Reuse loot tables across multiple containers.</li>
        <li>Keep reward configuration separate from container placement.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>


<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Assignment configuration file</h2>
  <p>
    Container assignments are stored in a JSON file inside your Minecraft
    configuration directory:
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">File location</div>
    <pre><code>config/lootcontainers/loot_tables/container_assignments.json</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    If the file does not exist when the assignment manager loads, the mod
    creates a default configuration automatically.
  </p>

  <h3>Default assignment</h3>
  <p>
    The default configuration creates an enabled assignment named
    <strong>Overworld Containers</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">Default assignment values</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "id": "overworld_containers",
  "displayName": "Overworld Containers",
  "blockId": "",
  "blockTag": "lootcontainers:loot_containers",
  "lootTableId": "overworld",
  "enabled": true
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    This assignment uses a block tag rather than an individual block ID
    and connects matching containers to the Overworld loot table.
  </p>
</div>


<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Assignment properties</h2>
  <p>
    Every container assignment contains several properties that determine
    which blocks it applies to and which loot table those blocks use.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrapper">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Property</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>id</code></td>
          <td>Unique identifier for the assignment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>displayName</code></td>
          <td>Name displayed for the assignment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>dimensionId</code></td>
          <td>Dimension where the assignment applies. Empty means any dimension.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>blockId</code></td>
          <td>Targets one specific block.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>blockTag</code></td>
          <td>Targets blocks belonging to a block tag.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>blockIds</code></td>
          <td>Targets multiple individually specified blocks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>lootTableId</code></td>
          <td>ID of the loot table used by this assignment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>enabled</code></td>
          <td>Determines whether the assignment is enabled.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Choosing a block selection method</h2>
  <p>
    Each assignment must use exactly one of the three available block
    selection methods. Combining them or leaving all three empty will
    cause validation to fail.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Individual block</h3>
  <p>
    Use <code>blockId</code> when you want an assignment to target one
    specific block.
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">Example</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "blockId": "minecraft:chest",
  "blockTag": "",
  "blockIds": []
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Block tag</h3>
  <p>
    Use <code>blockTag</code> when you want an assignment to target
    every block belonging to a particular tag.
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">Example</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "blockId": "",
  "blockTag": "lootcontainers:loot_containers",
  "blockIds": []
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Multiple block IDs</h3>
  <p>
    Use <code>blockIds</code> when you want one assignment to target
    several individually specified blocks.
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">Example</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "blockId": "",
  "blockTag": "",
  "blockIds": [
    "minecraft:chest",
    "minecraft:trapped_chest",
    "minecraft:barrel"
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>


<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Dimension matching</h2>
  <p>
    Assignments can optionally target a specific Minecraft dimension.
    This lets you use different loot tables for containers in the
    Overworld, Nether, End, or custom dimensions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <code>dimensionId</code> property accepts a Minecraft resource
    location, such as <code>minecraft:the_nether</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">Nether-only assignment</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "dimensionId": "minecraft:the_nether"
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="info-card">
    <h3>All dimensions</h3>
    <p>
      Leave <code>dimensionId</code> empty to allow the assignment
      to match in any dimension.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

  <h2>Validation rules</h2>
  <p>
    The assignment validator checks configurations before they can be
    saved. Invalid assignments are rejected.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Assignment IDs must contain only lowercase letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens.</li>
    <li>Assignment IDs must be unique.</li>
    <li>Dimension IDs must be valid resource locations when provided.</li>
    <li>Block IDs and block tags must be valid resource locations.</li>
    <li>Block ID lists cannot contain invalid or duplicate entries.</li>
    <li>Exactly one block selection method must be configured.</li>
    <li>The referenced loot table must exist in the loot table registry.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="info-card">
    <h3>Important</h3>
    <p>
      A valid assignment is not automatically active just because it exists
      in the JSON file. Its <code>enabled</code> property must also be true.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>


<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Assignment priority</h2>
  <p>
    Container assignments are evaluated in the order they appear in
    <code>container_assignments.json</code>. When multiple assignments
    could match the same block, the first matching assignment takes priority.
  </p>

  <div class="info-card">
    <h3>First matching assignment wins</h3>
    <p>
      Once an enabled assignment matches the block and dimension,
      the registry immediately returns its assigned loot table.
      Assignments below it are not evaluated.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>
    Imagine you have two assignments targeting the same chest:
  </p>

  <div class="code-card">
    <div class="code-heading">container_assignments.json</div>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "assignments": [
    {
      "id": "special_chests",
      "displayName": "Special Chests",
      "blockId": "minecraft:chest",
      "blockTag": "",
      "blockIds": [],
      "lootTableId": "rare_rewards",
      "enabled": true,
      "dimensionId": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "general_chests",
      "displayName": "General Chests",
      "blockId": "minecraft:chest",
      "blockTag": "",
      "blockIds": [],
      "lootTableId": "overworld",
      "enabled": true,
      "dimensionId": ""
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    In this example, a chest matches both assignments, but
    <code>special_chests</code> comes first. Therefore, it receives
    the <code>rare_rewards</code> loot table.
  </p>

  <h3>Changing assignment priority</h3>
  <p>
    You can change priority by moving assignments to different positions
    in the assignment list. The assignment manager supports moving an
    assignment to a new position and saving the updated configuration.
  </p>

  <h2>When no assignment matches</h2>
  <p>
    If the block does not match any enabled assignment, or if its dimension
    does not match the configured dimension, the registry returns no loot
    table for that block.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <code>hasAssignment</code> method can also be used to check whether
    a block has an enabled assignment that resolves to a loot table.
  </p>
</div>


<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Container interaction and rewards</h2>
  <p>
    When a player right-clicks an assigned container, the server handles
    the interaction and determines whether the player has already received
    a reward result from that specific container.
  </p>

  <h3>One reward attempt per player</h3>
  <p>
    Each container has a unique identity, and reward results are tracked
    separately for every player. Once a player has attempted to receive
    a reward from a container, that result is saved.
  </p>

  <div class="info-card">
    <h3>Failed attempts count too</h3>
    <p>
      A failed reward roll is recorded just like a successful one.
      Players cannot repeatedly interact with the same container to
      reroll until they receive a reward.
    </p>
  </div>


<h3>What happens when no reward is found?</h3>
<p>
  If the reward roll fails, the unsuccessful result is saved.
  The player cannot make another reward attempt from that same
  container.
</p>

<p>
  Unlike successful rewards, failed reward attempts do not cancel
  the original block interaction. Normal block interaction behavior
  can therefore continue.
</p>


  <h2>Container identity</h2>
  <p>
    Containers use unique UUIDs to distinguish individual placed containers.
    This allows the mod to maintain separate reward records even when
    multiple containers use the same block type and loot table.
  </p>

  <h3>Moving containers</h3>
  <p>
    The mod also supports restoring container identity when an assigned
    container is placed from an item that carries its saved identity.
    This helps preserve its existing tracking information when it is moved.
  </p>

  <h2>Important behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Only server-side main-hand right-click interactions are processed by this handler.</li>
    <li>Unassigned blocks are not processed by this reward handler.</li>
    <li>Each player has their own reward result for each container.</li>
    <li>Both successful and unsuccessful results are saved.</li>
    <li>Container records persist through Minecraft's SavedData system.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="doc-section">
  <h2>Per-player container tracking</h2>
  <p>
    Loot Containers tracks container interactions separately for each player.
    This allows the mod to remember which players have interacted with a
    particular container.
  </p>

  <h3>How tracking works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Container ID:</strong> Each tracked container has a unique UUID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Player ID:</strong> Each player is identified by their Minecraft UUID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opening records:</strong> The mod records whether a player has
      opened a particular container.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reward results:</strong> The mod separately records whether a
      player received a reward from that container.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="info-card">
    <h3>Why track players individually?</h3>
    <p>
      A container can keep separate interaction records for different players.
      This means one player's interaction does not automatically count as
      another player's interaction.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Saved container data</h2>
  <p>
    Container tracking is stored using Minecraft's SavedData system.
    The data storage identifier is
    <code>lootcontainers_data</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both opening records and reward results are written to world data and
    restored when that data is loaded again. This prevents the tracking
    information from simply disappearing when the world is restarted.
  </p>
</div>


</DocsLayout>
